From: wsclean-mx <wsclean-mx@localhost>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:39:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 01/19] WGridder::RealImage: zero the padding instead of NaN

wgridder_float.cpp and wgridder_double.cpp are compiled with -ffast-math.
Under it, clang drops std::vector<float>(n, quiet_NaN()) to a plain
allocation, so the border around the trimmed dirty image holds stale heap
data. With small inversion that border is resampled into the image, giving
intermittently corrupted dirty images (2048^2: 4 of 6 runs bad, others
contaminated at ~3% rms). The resampler zeroes non-finite input, so zero is
the intended value.

diff --git a/wgridder/wgridder_implementation.h b/wgridder/wgridder_implementation.h
index 34477e42..756dc6f0 100644
--- a/wgridder/wgridder_implementation.h
+++ b/wgridder/wgridder_implementation.h
@@ -308,7 +308,10 @@ template <typename NumT>
 std::vector<float> WGridder<NumT>::RealImage() {
   const size_t dx = (width_ - trimmed_width_) / 2;
   const size_t dy = (height_ - trimmed_height_) / 2;
-  std::vector<float> image(width_ * height_, std::numeric_limits<float>::quiet_NaN());
+  // This file is compiled with -ffast-math, under which clang drops a NaN fill
+  // and leaves the padding uninitialized. Zero is what the resampler turns
+  // non-finite padding into anyway.
+  std::vector<float> image(width_ * height_, 0.0f);
   for (size_t i = 0; i < trimmed_width_; ++i)
     for (size_t j = 0; j < trimmed_height_; ++j)
       image[(i + dx) + (j + dy) * width_] = image_[i * trimmed_height_ + j];
